[49.848, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I saved a copy of the plan in `.ambition/first/`. There's no `.gitignore` in this folder, so I didn't change\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  one.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Do you want to stop here or push the plan further?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **Stop here:** keep the plan as it is.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **Push it further:** I'd go after three things.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m     - **Your shop's real numbers:** I'd ask about your niche, prices, sales and budget, then replace the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  generic targets with ones that fit your shop.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m     - **Keyword research:** I'd run actual Etsy search research and write ready-to-use titles and tags for\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  your top listings.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m     - **Calendar and tracking:** I'd turn the 90-day plan into a dated content and promotion calendar, plus a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  simple tracking sheet.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m     The first item would take a few questions from you. The other two are about as much work as the original\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  plan, so roughly double what's been spent so far.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[54.669, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Push it further.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[58.855, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 2/8 · Altitude\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'll ask up to four questions, one at a time. The first is about the goal.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What is this plan ultimately for, and what should happen once it's done?** For example:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Replace a side income with a full-time one, which means a monthly revenue target.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Get ready for the next holiday season (Q4 is close).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Grow a young shop out of its first-sales slump.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Use the plan to decide whether to expand beyond Etsy, such as your own website or wholesale.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Tell me which is closest, or describe your own goal.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[64.207, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Closest is growing a young shop out of its first-sales slump. I've been open about a year, I have a handful\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  of listings that sell occasionally, but sales are uneven and I don't really know what's working. I'd like a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  plan that gets me to steady monthly sales, and the holiday season is the obvious first push.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[69.477, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Thanks, that changes the plan a lot. Today is mid-November, so the holiday push is already underway, and the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  plan has to start with finding out what's working, not with new channels.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What would make this plan unmistakably excellent for you?** I need a specific target. Examples:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Revenue:** a steady monthly figure, such as $1,000 or $3,000 a month by spring. Tell me your current\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  average and the number you want.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Order count:** at least N orders every month, with no near-empty months.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Holiday result:** a set sales goal for November and December, plus a plan that still holds after the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  rush.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Usability:** a plan you can follow in under 5 hours a week, with each week's tasks already written out.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Give me the numbers you have, even rough ones, and say how many hours a week you can spend.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[74.792, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ I'm averaging about $1,200 a month right now, with around 35 orders. The goal is to get to $3,000 a month by\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  spring, and I'd like a decent holiday bump on the way, say $2,000 in each of November and December. I can\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  put in about 5 hours a week alongside my day job. Please write out each week's tasks so I can just follow\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  them.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[78.42, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **What would prove the plan works, other than me saying so?** Your own shop data is the best judge. Pick the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  check you want:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Etsy Stats and your sales export:** a weekly scorecard (visits, conversion rate, orders, revenue)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  against the targets above. The plan would set a baseline from your last 12 months of data first.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A kill-or-keep rule:** each tactic gets a stated threshold. For example, an ad that doesn't return 2x\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  its spend in 3 weeks is cut.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Outside reference:** compare your listings against 5 to 10 top-ranking candle shops in your niche.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I can combine them. Can you export your Etsy data (Shop Manager → Settings → Options → Download Data →\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Orders and Listings CSVs) and share the files? That would be the main access I need.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[85.484, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Combine all three, that sounds right. A weekly scorecard plus a kill-or-keep rule is what I'd actually use,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  and the competitor comparison helps me see where I stand.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  I don't have the CSV exports handy, and I'd rather not share my shop files. Instead, here's what I can tell\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  you: I'm a small shop with about 25 listings, mostly soy candles in jars, priced around $18 to $32. I get\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  roughly 40 orders a month, mostly from Etsy search, and I spend nothing on ads yet. My budget for marketing\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  is about $150 a month, and I can put in maybe 5 hours a week. Please build the plan from that and mark the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  numbers as estimates I should replace with my real stats.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[102.148, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ You asked for a marketing plan for your Etsy candle shop. Here is the restated task. Please confirm it or\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  adjust it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  You asked for:   A marketing plan for my handmade candle shop on Etsy, as a Markdown file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Raises all of:   - Marketing plan: stays a plan in Markdown. It goes from a generic menu of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     tactics to a prioritised plan built around your numbers, with the tactics\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     that don't fit a ~25-listing shop and a $150/month budget cut.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Etsy fit: SEO, ads, social, email and partnerships stay in the plan. Each\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     one gets a budget, hours per week, and a kill-or-keep threshold.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Your shop: the plan is written for ~25 soy-jar listings at $18–32, selling\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     mostly through Etsy search, with no ad spend so far.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Genre bar:       A marketing plan is judged on whether someone can act on it Monday morning.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   That means a clear priority order, honest trade-offs, numbers that add up,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   and no filler. It should read like a consultant's plan, not a checklist\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   dump. It should be easy to scan and follow week by week, and it should say\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   plainly which advice is an estimate.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Aiming at:       Steady monthly sales, because your problem is uneven sales and not knowing\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   what's working. The plan starts by measuring (a baseline and a weekly\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   scorecard), then spends effort where the data says it pays.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Excellent means: - Targets: $2,000 in each of November and December, and $3,000/month by\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     spring. The plan shows the arithmetic (orders × average order value) and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     which levers close the gap. At your ~$30–34 average order, $3,000 means\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     ~90 orders a month, or ~70 if the average order rises to ~$43 through\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     bundles and gift sets. Both routes are shown.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Time and budget: no week needs more than 5 hours, and the total spend\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     stays within $150/month.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Week-by-week tasks: every week, from now to spring, has a dated task list\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     with hours estimated for each task. November and December are mapped\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     week by week, and January onward is laid out as a steady cycle.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Scorecard: a weekly scorecard sheet (visits, conversion rate, orders,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     revenue, ad return) with your estimated baseline marked \"replace with real\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     stats\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Kill-or-keep rules: every tactic has a stated threshold and review date.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Competitor comparison: a table of 5–10 comparable candle shops (price\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     points, photo count, reviews, listing count, titles/tags), with the gaps\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     it shows for your shop.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Honesty: all figures that come from your description, not from Etsy data,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     are labelled estimates. The plan states the mismatch between the two order\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     counts you gave (35 and ~40) and uses ~35–40 as the baseline range.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Proven by:       1. Arithmetic check: a script that recomputes every revenue, order and budget\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      figure in the plan from its stated assumptions, and confirms the weekly\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      hours and monthly spend stay within 5 hours and $150.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   2. Competitor data: the comparison table is built from live listings I read,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      not from memory. Etsy may block automated page reads. If it does, I'll\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      say so and mark the gaps instead of making numbers up.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   3. Your own use: the weekly scorecard and kill-or-keep rules are the check\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      you run on the plan. Each rule names the number you read off Etsy Stats.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   4. Against the first version: I'll compare the result with the first version\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      on the same points (specificity, actionability, whether the numbers add\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      up) and keep anything the first version did better.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   5. Reading it through: I'll read the finished file start to finish for flow\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      and for filler.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Needs access to: - Web search/fetch for the competitor comparison. Etsy listing pages may\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     block it, in which case I'll use what's publicly readable.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Your real Etsy stats, which you've chosen not to share. So the plan is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     built on estimates and marked clearly where you swap in real numbers.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Mode:            build · answerer: you · reviewer: none (no per-step review requested)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Two things to settle:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **Holiday timing.** It's November 13, so the Q4 push is already late. The plan treats the next few weeks\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  as a sprint (what can still pay off before the shipping cutoffs) and sets up January–spring after that. Is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  that right?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **Order count.** You said ~35 orders earlier and ~40 now. I'll use 35–40 and treat the average order\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  value as ~$30–34. Is that fine?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Reply \"go\" if the restatement is fine, or tell me what to change.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[107.153, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Yes, both are right. Treat the next few weeks as a holiday sprint focused on what can still pay off before\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  the shipping cutoffs, then set up January through spring. Use 35–40 orders as the baseline range and an\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  average order of about $30–34. Go, and please finish the whole plan without stopping.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
